# v0.25.9 — post-v0.25.8-tag: bridge payout monitor, governance service-local reclassification, v9 shadow window

**Baseline**: `v0.25.8` tagged at `6026404775` (2026-09-29). Sections below accrued on `main` after the tag and are relocated here per the release-tag policy (`AGENTS.md`: the version's changelog is finalised in the tagged commit; later entries go to the next version's log).
**Date**: 2026-09-30.
**Tag**: `v0.25.9` (pending).


### Governance-vote casing follow-up: canonicalize at write + per-host NOCASE index (2026-09-29)

Follow-up to the dedupe above. The twins existed because
``cast_governance_vote`` lowercased ``voter_address`` before storing while
aux upserts arrived checksummed — two write spellings for one identity.

- ``rpc/staking.py``: every request-supplied address the staking/governance
  routes store now goes through ``_request_address`` (0x-prefix if missing,
  then EIP-55 via ``_to_ait_address``): ``voter_address`` in
  ``cast_governance_vote``, ``proposer_address`` in
  ``create_governance_proposal``, ``agent_address`` in
  ``register_agent_identity``, ``verified_by`` in ``verify_agent_identity``
  (verbatim before — now ``_to_ait_address`` passthrough/canonical), and the
  ``stake.address`` insert in ``stake_tokens``. Reads that meet pre-fix
  lowercase rows compare case-insensitively (``get_staking_info``,
  the vote-power sum, the ``stake.address`` ownership check in
  ``unstake_tokens``, and the already-voted check — which would otherwise
  miss a legacy lowercase row and hand the insert to the new index as a
  500 instead of the intended 400).
- Signed-message contract preserved: ``sign_data["address"]`` in
  ``stake_tokens``/``unstake_tokens`` keeps the *lowercase* spelling because
  the wallet CLI signs ``hex_address.lower()`` while submitting the
  checksummed form — canonicalizing inside the signed message would break
  every existing client signature. Storage, the queued transfer's
  ``signer``, and responses carry the canonical form instead.
- User-visible impact that existed: ``GET /governance/proposal/{id}``
  answered ``total_votes=len(votes)`` — 10 on node2 vs 5 on the rest of the
  fleet for the same proposal while the twins were live. Resolved by the
  dedupe; prevented from recurring by the write canonicalization + index.

Schema-migration note (per-host, applied 2026-09-29): SQLModel does not
backfill indexes onto existing databases, so the case-insensitive
uniqueness belt was applied by hand on all five fleet hosts after the
dup-scan returned 0 rows everywhere:

    SELECT chain_id, proposal_id, voter_address COLLATE NOCASE, COUNT(*)
      FROM governance_vote GROUP BY 1,2,3 HAVING COUNT(*)>1;   -- 0 on all 5
    CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ux_governance_vote_voter_nocase
      ON governance_vote(chain_id, proposal_id, voter_address COLLATE NOCASE);

Any new host/chain DB needs the same statement — it is not in the model
``__table_args__`` (sqlite ``COLLATE NOCASE`` in an index is dialect-bound)
and ``create_all`` will not emit it.

Tests: ``test_governance_vote_canonicalization.py`` — canonical storage at
the vote route, either-spelling stake power lookup, legacy-lowercase
revote blocked with 400, twin insert rejected by the NOCASE index, the
dup-scan returning clean, and the lowercase signed-message contract pinned
both ways (lowercase verifies and stores canonical; canonical-signed is
rejected — the pre-existing CLI contract).

### Escrow routing: 404 is submit-time divergence, not corruption (2026-09-29)

The ``escrow`` table is service-local: a create/release/refund routed to a
node other than the one that accepted the create gets a 404 "Escrow not
found" — expected divergence, not corruption and not a sync failure.
Confirmed the missing-row routes return 404 as designed
(``rpc/routers/settlement.py``: :181, :280, :317; refund maps the
service-level miss at :160). Routing rule + design TODO recorded in
``docs/ops/escrow-routing.md``: aux service tables should be *derivable
from sealed ``ESCROW_*`` history* — the chain already records the
lock/release/refund transitions, so a node could rebuild its escrow view by
replay instead of depending on which node fielded the submit POST.

### bridge_validators → height-scoped chain state: feasibility note (2026-09-29)

Why the current design diverges per node:

- ``bridge_validators`` is written only by the RPC
  (``POST /bridge/validators/register`` → ``register_validator``) into the
  node's own service-local table — nothing ships it through consensus or
  aux sync, so each node's set is whatever was POSTed to it.
- ``confirm_transfer`` → ``_validate_proof`` counts recovered signers
  against that local set (``_verify_header_threshold_signatures`` →
  ``check_threshold``), and ``bridge_release_enabled`` fails closed when no
  set is registered — so the same proof can confirm on one node and fail
  on another. Bridge confirmation outcome is node-dependent today.

Fix direction: the validator set belongs in chain state, resolvable at a
height — the same mechanism as ``bridge_release_authority``, which reads
``chain_parameter_history`` via ``_chain_parameter_value`` so every node
resolves the identical value at a given block
(``state_transition._bridge_release_authority``). A
``bridge_validator_set`` chain parameter (JSON set + threshold + epoch,
height-scoped) gives the confirm path a deterministic membership set.

Signature-domain finding (the important part): the collected
``remote_attestation`` attestations already sign *exactly* the message the
bridge header path verifies. Both sides serialize
``{chain_id, height, hash, parent_hash, proposer, state_root,
bridge_state_root}`` as canonical JSON → keccak256 → secp256k1 —
``sign_consensus_message`` / ``verify_block_signature`` on the attestation
side, ``build_verification_message`` + ``recover_all_signers`` on the
bridge side. No domain separation exists between the two, so a collected
attestation is *directly usable* as a ``validator_signatures`` entry — no
dual-signing, no proof-format change for the header path. The transfer
binding does not need the signature to cover the transfer fields: the
proof's merkle inclusion into ``bridge_state_root`` already binds the
transfer to the signed header (``_validate_proof`` step 5a/5c).

The gap, precisely: attestations do **not** cover the legacy non-header
multisig domain (``_verify_threshold_signatures`` without a header hashes
the whole proof dict minus signature fields). If that path ever becomes
live, either attesters must dual-sign the proof dict, or the proof must
carry/embed the block hash and take the header path — it already carries
``block_hash``/``block_height`` and ``_validate_proof`` materializes the
header from them, so the header path is the natural one. Also worth noting:
the shared domain means a consensus attestation is replayable as a bridge
header signature — same statement ("V signed header H"), so harmless, but
a domain tag would be the cleaner long-term cut if the two diverge.

And (d): threshold ≥2 is unreachable today regardless — nothing populates
``validator_signatures``. ``collect_attestations`` gathers validator
signatures at proposal time and discards them after use; bridge proofs
carry only ``proposer_signature``, and ``bridge_block_header.signature``
stores a single signature. The 1-signer proposer path
(``_verify_proposer_signature`` / the non-multisig branch of
``_verify_header_threshold_signatures``, requiring the recovered signer ==
``header.proposer`` and ∈ the validator set) is the only working path.

### New bridge locks paused — BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED (2026-09-29)

``POST /bridge/lock``, ``/bridge/batch/lock``, ``/swap`` and
``/cross-chain/bridge`` now return HTTP 503
``{"detail": "Bridge paused: locks unavailable until validator attestation is live"}``
while ``BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED=true`` (``config.py: bridge_locks_paused``,
code default False; set true in ``/etc/aitbc/blockchain.env`` on all five
hosts — the env file the ``aitbc-blockchain-rpc`` unit already loads, so
the RPC process and the in-process bridge see the flag). Refund
(``/bridge/unlock``), status reads, confirm, batch-confirm and withdraw
are deliberately untouched: in-flight transfers stay refundable/releasable
while locks are paused.

Boundary chosen: the lock *request* path, not mempool admission. All four
REST surfaces funnel through ``CrossChainBridge.initiate_transfer``, which
now raises ``BridgeLocksPausedError`` (a ValueError) before any
debit/row/mempool write — the chokepoint also covers internal callers, so
no new validly-signed BRIDGE_LOCK envelope can be created while paused.
Admission stays open on purpose: nothing outside ``initiate_transfer``
can produce a BRIDGE_LOCK that seals anyway (v9 apply requires the bridge
authority ``bridge_signature`` — ``state/state_transition.py`` and the
pure path enforce it, and the proposer skips pre-registered locks whose
signature fails), a replayed envelope dedupes on ``tx_hash``, and — the
deciding point — a lock issued *before* the pause must still be able to
seal, because ``BRIDGE_REFUND`` binds a *sealed* lock since v6. Refusing
admission while paused would strand an in-flight lock's funds, which is
exactly what the pause exists to prevent.

Verified before enabling: ``cross_chain_transfer`` has zero pending/locked
rows on all five hosts (only confirmed + refunded), and no BRIDGE_LOCK
sits in any mempool — nothing in flight to drain.

### Bridge validator set — decision: the 4 consensus validators, height-scoped (2026-09-29)

Operator decision on the bridge confirm validator set (builds on the
2026-09-29 feasibility note above, which established that collected
attestations already sign exactly the header domain the bridge verifies):

- The ``bridge_validators`` table is retired for confirm purposes. The
  validator set becomes the 4 consensus validators, stored as a
  height-scoped chain parameter — the same mechanism as
  ``bridge_release_authority`` (``chain_parameter_history`` via
  ``_chain_parameter_value``), so every node resolves the identical set
  at a given block height.
- Threshold is 3-of-4, matching block production (proposer +
  ``min_attestations=2``): a block is final exactly when the bridge's
  release quorum is met, so the confirm threshold becomes reachable —
  ``bridge_multisig_threshold=2`` is unreachable today because stored
  headers carry only the proposer signature.
- The proof is the block header plus its stored attestations verified
  against that set. Attestations sign the header domain including
  ``bridge_state_root`` and the proof binds the block hash — feasible
  per the earlier note; a dedicated domain tag remains the cleaner
  long-term cut if consensus and bridge verification diverge.
- The ETH contract validator list and threshold are updated in the same
  change as the key split.

Until that lands, new locks stay paused (see the BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED
entry): locking value behind an unreachable release path would only park
funds.

### BOND_*/RECEIPT_CLAIM parity fixes verified behavior-neutral (2026-09-29)

Fleet-wide verification for the parallel-path parity fixes landed in
6026404775 (``feat(sync): … pure/sequential parity``): the ``transaction``
table holds zero ``BOND_*`` rows and zero ``RECEIPT_CLAIM`` rows ever
sealed on every host, so the BOND_* sequential-only correction and the
RECEIPT_CLAIM stale-ORM fix are behavior-neutral on all recorded history.
They change only what a *future* block containing such a transaction
would compute; no past block exercises either path, so no replay
divergence is possible.

### Attester re-signs the already-imported head — not_at_parent false positive (2026-09-29)

Attestation requests can arrive *after* the block they name has already
been imported on a fast follower: the request's ``parent_hash`` then
mismatches the local head because the head IS the requested block. Seen
live on node1 — "Import block check 27718" at 08:13:18, then "would
refuse … not_at_parent" at 08:13:21 for the same hash. At v9 that refusal
would have cost the block an attestation it had earned.

``consensus/remote_attestation.py::_v9_check_transactions`` now treats
``tip.height == request.height and tip.hash == request.hash`` — i.e. the
local head IS the requested block — as attestable and returns before the
parent-binding check: import already ran every tx-level validation on
this exact block, so re-signing it is idempotent. The tx-binding
recomputes (``unbound_tx_set``/``tx_count_mismatch``/``tx_set_mismatch``)
still run first, so a request cannot claim the head's hash without
committing to its real transaction set. Fork protection is unchanged: a
rival hash at the same height falls through to ``not_at_parent`` exactly
as before (tip hash differs from the request hash), and a block ahead of
the head still runs the full checks.

Tests added under ``TestAttesterTxChecks`` in
``tests/test_v9_signature_rules.py``: (a) a request arriving after import
signs with no ``v9_would_reject`` counter; (b) a rival hash at the head's
height still refuses and counts; (c) a not-yet-imported block ahead of
the head still runs the tx checks.
### governance_proposal reclassified to service-local (2026-09-29)

Operator review finding: ``governance_proposal`` is not consensus state.
GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE at apply is gated on the executor signature alone — it
marks the stored proposal ``executed`` and applies the tx-carried
``execution_payload`` (an override, not bound to the proposed payload);
the proposal's tally/status are never read at apply. Proposals, votes,
and tallies are RPC-side bookkeeping written on whichever node served the
call (``rpc/staking.py::cast_governance_vote`` takes ``voter_address``
unsigned and updates the tally — an operator-administrative model, not
on-chain governance).

Changes:

- ``governance_proposal`` moved from CONSENSUS_STATE_TABLES to
  SERVICE_STATE_TABLES and dropped from ``aux_state.AUX_TABLES`` /
  AUX_SHIPPED_TABLES — it is no longer shipped between nodes, and the
  digest monitor classifies it ``service`` (informational). Without this
  every RPC-side vote/proposal would read as permanent aux divergence
  while state sync stays disabled.
- Corrected the earlier audit comment that claimed tallies gate
  execution.
- Executor gate itself verified safe today, correcting the audit worry:
  ``governance_executors`` is SET on all five hosts (0x02B8F2C6…, applied
  at block 9819 via gap56-set-executors) and apply already fails closed
  when the parameter is unset for block_version >= 5
  (state_transition.py:1064) — the "unset means unrestricted" docstring
  was stale, the enforcement was not.
- Governance canaries (27262 GOVERNANCE_PROPOSE, 27263 GOVERNANCE_VOTE)
  were sealed transactions — but apply writes no proposal/vote rows for
  them; they exercised signature+nonce admission only, not tally or
  execution. Recorded so the canary record is accurate.
- Version-height work queued: bind ``execution_payload`` to the stored
  proposal (apply what was proposed, not what the tx carries), and — if
  on-chain governance is intended — signed GOVERNANCE_VOTE txs with
  stake-snapshot tallies and execution gated on the tally. Also noted:
  ``governance_vote`` keeps class ``aux`` (must-match) deliberately —
  its ledger divergence is the sync-contract signal that caught the
  v0.25.8 case-duplicates; revisit alongside proposal if alert noise
  appears from RPC-side votes while sync is off.
### governance_executors gate verified and pinned (2026-09-29)

Operator-ordered check, outcome: **no emergency execute needed** —
``governance_executors`` is set on all five hosts
(``0x02B8F2C61DB19B04aB68cfb43d0605E63dE74c5B``, applied at block 9819 via
``gap56-set-executors``; identical values verified by direct query and the
digest monitor).

Empirical gate verification (in-memory harness on node2):

- funded non-executor ``GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE`` (parameter_change →
  bridge_release_authority=attacker): REJECTED at v5/v6/v7/v8 —
  "not an authorized executor".
- executor sender: passes.
- executor list UNSET: REJECTED at v5+ ("parameter is not set" — the
  fail-closed branch). Lenient only at v4, where sealed history predates
  the parameter — the intended compatibility path.
- chain_parameter_history audit: 4 authority-parameter changes total, all
  gap56–gap60 proposals to known addresses — every change accounted for.

New regression test ``tests/test_governance_executor_gate.py`` (4 cases)
pins the gate so it cannot silently regress.

Queued version-height work (unchanged): bind ``execution_payload`` to the
stored proposal rather than the tx-carried override, and — if on-chain
governance is intended — signed votes with stake-snapshot tallies gating
execution. The executor key remains the most sensitive key: it can
reassign every authority, including itself — first in the key-split list.
### Key-split ordering — all authority resolves to one key (2026-09-29)

The parameter-history audit confirms every authority lands on hub's
``0x02B8F2C6…`` key: ``governance_executors`` (9819),
``escrow_settlement_authority`` (22193), ``bridge_release_authority``
(22469) — only ``bond_slash_authority`` differs (``0xab0797…``).

Split order, ranked by the damage each key can do:

1. **governance_executors** — can reassign every other authority,
   including itself. New dedicated offline key; switched via
   ``parameter_change`` executed by the current executor (last use of
   0x02B8… for governance).
2. **bridge_release_authority** — signs credits that mint funds. New key;
   same change carries the bridge validator-set parameter (4 consensus
   validators, 3-of-4) and the ETH contract validator list+threshold, so
   the contract never points at keys being rotated.
3. **escrow_settlement_authority** — settles locks/refunds.
4. **bond_slash_authority** — already on a separate key; verify it is not
   on a running host.
Plus the non-parameter items: faucet gets its own funded wallet (~1 week
budget); treasury/genesis funds move to an offline address; the proposer
key stays confined to aitbc-blockchain-node only.

